<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Lycian dynasts</h1><p class="st-meta">540 BCE – 360 BCE (approx.) · capital Xanthos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Lycian dynasts, local rulers of Xanthos and other cities who governed Lycia under Persian overlordship and built striking tombs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In c. 540 BCE the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> conquered Lycia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells that the men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthos</a> killed their families and died fighting. Afterwards a dynasty at Xanthos, perhaps descended from Harpagus, and rival dynasts in other cities ruled Lycia as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasts minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins with Lycian legends and raised pillar tombs and temple-tombs, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nereid-monument">Nereid Monument</a> of Erbbina, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. The Inscribed Pillar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthos</a> carries the longest known Lycian text. Lycian culture blended Anatolian traditions with Greek art.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 4th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limyra">Limyra</a> briefly united Lycia and joined the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus of Hecatomnid Caria</a> took control c. 360 BCE. The dynasts disappeared, and Lycia later reorganised as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lycian-league">Lycian League</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 540 BCE – 360 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:48.96%;width:3.58%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>540 BCE – 360 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xanthos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kheriga, Erbbina, Pericles of Limyra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lycian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lycian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lycia, Dynasty of Xanthos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=lycian-dynasts" aria-label="Lycian dynasts on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5418 866 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lycian dynasts lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5738 1122L6140 1251M5738 1122L5591 1068M5738 1122L5687 1100"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5687 1100h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5738 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5738 1122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Xanthos. <a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=lycian-dynasts">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

